---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { aboutPageContent } from "../config";
import SocialLinkBox from "../components/SocialLinkBox.astro";
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
---

<Layout seo={aboutPageContent.seo}>
  <section class="mt-10 max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl mb-1">About Me</h1>
    <p class="opacity-60 mb-10 subtitle">Hey — glad you found your way here. Read on.</p>
    <div class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xl mb-6">Origin story</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <p>
          I'm a lifelong technologist. My first steps into computing came in the
          early 1990s, before the internet had become part of everyday life in
          Australia.
        </p>
        <p>
          I was lucky to grow up in a household with a computer. An IBM machine
          sat in the entryway of our family home: a heavy power switch, a screen
          flickering through its boot sequence, and eventually the familiar
          <code>C:\&gt;</code> prompt. It felt like a world of possibilities.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was a DOS world. Windows 3.1 was the new kid on the block, software
          was built by wizards, games arrived on floppy disks, and the
          three-finger salute (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>Del</kbd>)
          became a reflex. It is hard to imagine what we tolerated, but it
          made every discovery feel earned.
        </p>
        <p>
          My dad set the wheels in motion. He was not a computer enthusiast, but
          he knew his way around one, and that was enough to make the machine
          approachable. Curiosity did the rest. I found
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BASIC" target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline-hover">BASIC</a>
          and started learning what happened when I replaced passive use with instructions
          of my own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Games added fuel to that curiosity. Before finishing primary school, I
          was repurposing discarded i486 and Pentium II machines, wiring them
          together with null-modem cables or cheap network cards, and hosting
          games of Command &amp; Conquer: Red Alert for my brother and friends.
          Those machines were unreliable, underpowered, and endlessly useful as
          teachers.
        </p>
        <p>
          By high school I was building my own computers and trying different
          programming languages. C++ was the one that made the pieces click into
          place. Like every intrepid young programmer, I decided I would build a
          game.
        </p>
        <p>
          That early pattern never really changed: understand a system by
          building something with it, follow the failure until it makes sense,
          and keep enough technical depth to turn an idea into a shipped thing.
          The systems are larger now, and the work is increasingly about helping
          teams do the same, but the underlying motivation is familiar.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row">
      <Image
        id="about-image-left"
        src={aboutPageContent.about.image_l.url}
        alt={aboutPageContent.about.image_l.alt}
        width={350}
        height={250}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        quality={100}
        densities={[1.5, 2]}
        class="w-[350px] h-[250px] object-cover object-top rounded-xl sm:-rotate-6 sm:block image-effect"
      />
      <Image
        id="about-image-right"
        src={aboutPageContent.about.image_r.url}
        alt={aboutPageContent.about.image_r.alt}
        width={150}
        height={250}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        quality={100}
        densities={[1.5, 2]}
        class="hidden w-[150px] h-[250px] object-cover rounded-xl rotate-6 mx-auto sm:ml-auto sm:block image-effect"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl mb-4">Engineering and leadership</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p>
        My work sits at the intersection of technical depth and engineering
        leadership. I enjoy finding practical paths through complicated
        product and systems problems, then giving the people around the work
        enough context, trust, and support to move quickly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Managing technical teams is where I thrive. I care about continued
        growth, clear ownership, and results that survive contact with the
        real world. I also intend to stay hands-on: two decades of coding have
        made software construction part of how I think, not a rung I wanted to
        leave behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside technology, the recurring interests are motorsport, family,
        gardening, and an unreasonable amount of reading. Learning is only
        useful when it changes what I build or how I lead, so I try to turn
        what I consume into experiments, tools, and better questions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="my-20 mt-32 max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6">
    <div
      class="p-5 sm:flex sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:gap-6 opacity-80 hover:!opacity-100 transition-opacity border border-dashed border-gray-300 rounded-lg"
    >
      <div class="mb-4 sm:mb-0">
        <h2 class="font-medium mb-1">Currently</h2>
        <div class="text-sm opacity-60">
          <p>
          Senior Manager, Software Engineering at <strong>GoPro</strong>, based
          in <strong>Sydney, Australia</strong>.
          </p>
          <br/>
          <p>
            My day to day is largely occupied with building and shipping product with a small team, obsessing over Kotlin Multiplatform, thinking about systems design, and reading an
            embarrassing amount. <i>See my recent career experience for more</i>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <a
        href="/recently"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 underline-hover shrink-0"
      >
        View Recently
        <div class="rotate-45">
          <Icon name="mdi:arrow-up" width={18} height={18} />
        </div>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-32 max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6 sm:flex sm:gap-16">
    <h2 class="mb-8 sm:mb-0 opacity-60">Connect</h2>
    <div>
      <div class="mb-6">
        <p>
          I'm heads-down at GoPro, but always happy to compare notes with people
        working on engineering leadership, Kotlin Multiplatform, developer
        tooling, or systems design.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 group">
        {
          aboutPageContent.connect.links.map((item) => (
            <SocialLinkBox {...item} />
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
